---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { href } from '../utils/url'

const title = 'About this list'
const description =
  'What this collection of paper notes is, how it is organised, and how to read it.'
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <div class="wrap paper-head">
    <a class="back-link" href={href()}>&larr; All papers</a>
    <h1>About this list</h1>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="prose">
      {/*
        TODO (yours to fill in): a short paragraph about who you are — what you
        work on, where, and what you are looking for. This page is the one a CV
        reader will click, so it is worth two or three honest sentences. Add a
        contact line too if you want to be reachable.
      */}

      <p>
        I read a lot of papers on interpretability and AI safety, and I kept
        losing what I had learned from them. My notes lived in a spreadsheet and
        an Obsidian vault, in two languages, in a form nobody else could read - not so convenient.
        This site is that material rewritten (always WIP) so it stays useful — to me and to anyone working on the same problems.
      </p>

      <h2>What is here and what is not</h2>

      <p>
        These are papers I found worth the time. It is not a survey and it is not
        balanced: there is far more on the geometry of representation spaces than
        on anything else, because that is what I work on right now and what I find
        interesting.
      </p>

      <p>
        Each entry separates what the paper claims from what I think about it. The
        question, the contributions, how it was tested and the headline results
        describe the paper. <strong>My notes</strong> and <strong>limitations</strong>
        are my own reading, and some of them are arguments the authors would
        disagree with.
      </p>

      <h2>How to read it</h2>

      <p>
        The reading path on the front page is five papers in the order that makes
        one argument, and it is the fastest way in. Otherwise the filters at the
        top narrow the list by theme, and the search box covers everything in every
        entry, including the parts collapsed by default.
      </p>

      <p>
        Papers with a longer write-up have their own page.
        The <a href={href() + '#reading-list'}>reading list</a> at the bottom is
        papers I am tracking but have not written up.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>

      <p>
        If I have got something wrong about your paper, or anyone's paper, I would
        like to know. Corrections are welcome through the
        <a href="https://github.com/Stacey-bubi/Stacey-bubi.github.io">repository</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
